# Specifying Targets

Nmap accepts a single host, multiple targets, an IP range, a subnet, or a file containing targets.

```bash
# Multiple targets
nmap MACHINE_IP scanme.nmap.org example.com

# IP range
nmap 10.11.12.15-20

# Subnet
nmap MACHINE_IP/30

# Read targets from a file
nmap -iL list_of_hosts.txt
```

Use `-sL` to list targets without scanning their ports:

```bash
nmap -sL TARGETS
```

Nmap may perform reverse-DNS lookups while listing targets. Add `-n` to disable DNS resolution:

```bash
nmap -sL -n TARGETS
```

---

# Host Discovery Only

By default, Nmap performs host discovery and then scans ports on hosts it considers online. Use `-sn` to discover live hosts without performing a port scan.

```bash
nmap -sn TARGETS
```

The discovery method can be selected or adjusted with `-PR`, `-PE`, `-PP`, `-PM`, `-PS`, `-PA`, and `-PU`. Results depend on network location, firewall rules, privileges, and the probes used. A host that does not respond is not necessarily offline.

---

# ARP Discovery

ARP discovery is especially useful when scanning hosts on the same local Ethernet/Wi-Fi subnet. A device needs the destination MAC address to deliver local network traffic, so ARP requests can reveal responding hosts.

```bash
sudo nmap -PR -sn 10.200.6.0/24
```

- `-PR`: use ARP discovery
- `-sn`: host discovery only

ARP is generally not routed across networks, so it is not the method for discovering hosts on a remote subnet.

