# TCP Host Discovery

TCP probes can be useful when ICMP is filtered.

## TCP SYN Ping

Nmap sends TCP SYN probes to selected ports. A SYN/ACK may indicate an open port; an RST may indicate a closed port. Either response can show that the host is reachable.

```bash
sudo nmap -PS22,80,443 -sn 10.200.6.0/30
```

Privileged users can generally send raw SYN probes. Unprivileged operation may use a different connection method depending on the platform and Nmap configuration.

## TCP ACK Ping

Nmap sends TCP ACK probes to selected ports. Because an unsolicited ACK is not part of an established connection, a reachable TCP stack commonly responds with RST.

```bash
sudo nmap -PA22,80,443 -sn 10.200.6.0/30
```

Firewall behaviour affects whether these probes receive a response.

---

# UDP Host Discovery

UDP probes can reveal whether a host is reachable. A closed UDP port may return an ICMP Port Unreachable message, while an open UDP port may not respond.

```bash
sudo nmap -PU53,161,162 -sn 10.200.6.0/30
```

A lack of response is ambiguous: the port may be open, the service may not reply, or a firewall may be filtering traffic.

---

# Reverse DNS

Reverse DNS (rDNS) looks up a hostname associated with an IP address. It can sometimes reveal host roles or naming conventions, but records may be missing or outdated.

```bash
# Force reverse-DNS lookups
nmap -R -sn TARGETS

# Disable DNS resolution
nmap -n -sn TARGETS

# Use a specific DNS server
nmap --dns-servers DNS_SERVER -sn TARGETS
```

- `-R`: resolve names for all targets, including hosts that may not be online
- `-n`: skip DNS resolution
- `--dns-servers`: specify DNS servers for Nmap to use

DNS lookups can add time to a scan and should be treated as clues rather than definitive asset identification.

